-- title:
--   Atkin–Lehner element ̄ w in the endomorphism dictionary
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Two distinct primes $r$ and $\bar r$ (hypothesis `hrr`), a positive integer $N$ that is squarefree (`hN`) and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrN`, `hrbarN`), and an integer $n \ge 3$ (`hn`) coprime to $N$ (`hnN`) and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrn`, `hrbarn`).
--
--   Coefficient rings. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`) with an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), whose residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`) and in which $(r) = (\pi)$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K₀$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Further, a characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal O$, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), with that ideal maximal (`hOnr_max`), such that every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`) and every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`). The base field throughout is $k_0 := Onr/(\pi)$.
--
--   Quaternionic data. Rationals $a,b$ with `hB` asserting that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ satisfies $0<a$ or $0<b$ and that its completion at a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ contains $r$ or $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ in it (`hΛ`, i.e. an order not properly contained in any order) containing all rational integers (`hΛℤ`); and a map `coord` $\colon \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r) \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r)$ satisfying `IsOrderCoord` (`hcoord`): additive, sending $1$ to $(1,0)$, multiplicative for the Frobenius-twisted product law, injective, with dense image modulo every power of $r$, and computing traces in the first coordinate. Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef` asserting $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is ramified exactly at $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) with $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0 \colon \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K₀)$; a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$; and a subgroup $\tilde\Gamma$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ which coincides elementwise (`hΓt`) with [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), the intersection over all height-one primes $w \ne v$ of the pullbacks along `toLoc w` of the closures of the local box units of $R_1$ at $w$.
--
--   Geometric data. A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $k_0$ with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of dimension $2$ over $k_0$ (so a two-dimensional commutative formal group with an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r)$ and a Frobenius-semilinear $\varpi$) of height $4$ (`hX₀`, i.e. the kernel of multiplication by $r$ has degree $r^4$), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, and `hA₀`: $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the relative group law of $A_0$ with formal group $X_0.F$, and the action of each $m \in \Lambda$ is computed on these coordinates by $X_0.\mathrm{act}$ of the first component of $\mathrm{coord}\,m$ added via $X_0.F$ to $X_0.\mathrm{act}$ of the second component composed with $\varpi$. Also given: maps $e, e' \colon \tilde\Gamma \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ with $e\gamma$ over the base (`he`), and degrees $\deg \colon \tilde\Gamma \to \mathbb N$.
--
--   The $\tilde\Gamma$-dictionary hypotheses. `hE1`: for each $\gamma$, the pair $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ from $A_0$ to itself (both maps are homomorphisms for the relative group law, both commute with the $\Lambda$-action, and the two composites are multiplication by $r^{\deg\gamma}$) and $e\gamma$ preserves the level structure. `hE1mul`: multiplicativity of $\gamma \mapsto e\gamma$ up to composition with multiplication by powers of $r$ on both sides. `hE1sc`: if $\gamma$ is the rational integer scalar $c$, then $e\gamma$ composed with $[r^i]$ equals $[c\,r^i]$ for some $i$. `hE1'`: conversely, if $e\gamma$ becomes a positive integer multiplication after composing with some $[r^i]$, then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar. `hE2`, the endomorphism dictionary: for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of $\{X_0.\mathrm{actEnd}\} \cup \{X_0.\mathrm{varpiEnd}\}$ inside $\operatorname{End}(X_0.F)$ into $M_2(K₀)$ there is a single $g \in GL_2(K₀)$ such that for every $\gamma$ there are $\varepsilon$ in that centraliser and $k_\gamma \in \mathbb Z$ with: $\varepsilon$ induces $e\gamma$ on formal points, i.e. for every $k_0$-algebra $B'$, every ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$ and every $s \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to J$ one has $\theta_0\,B'$ of the truncated evaluation of $\varepsilon$'s power series at $s$ equal to the image of $\theta_0\,B'(s)$ under $e\gamma$; and $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma}\, g\, \iota_0(\gamma)\, g^{-1}$. `hE3`: every full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ admits a labelling $\mathrm{lab} \colon \tilde\Gamma \to \Lambda$ with $e\gamma$ acting on $P_0$ as the $\Lambda$-action of $\mathrm{lab}\,\gamma$, a cocycle identity $\mathrm{lab}(\gamma\gamma') - \mathrm{lab}(\gamma')\,\mathrm{lab}(\gamma) \in n\Lambda$, and $\mathrm{lab}\,\gamma \equiv c \pmod{n\Lambda}$ whenever $\gamma$ is the integer scalar $c$. `hE4`: for every algebraically closed field $k$ over $k_0$, every fake elliptic curve $A$ over $k$ and every $g_A \colon A.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting $A$ as the pullback of $A_0$ along $k_0 \to k$, every level-preserving self-isogeny pair of $r$-power degree on $A$ is, after composing with powers of $r$ and transporting along $g_A$, of the form $e\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$.
--
--   The $R_2$-dictionary hypotheses. An order $R_2 \le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) such that every element of $R_1$ enters $R_2$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂r`), together with $\hat e \colon R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base (`hê`) satisfying: `hE5a` (each $\hat e x$ is a homomorphism for the group law, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level), `hE5one` ($\hat e 1 = \mathrm{id}$), `hE5mul` (contravariant multiplicativity), `hE5int` ($\hat e$ of a rational integer $m$ is multiplication by $m$), `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x)$ followed by $\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by the reduced norm of $x$), `hE5d` (if $x = r^k\gamma$ in $R_2$ then $e\gamma$ and $\hat e x$ agree after composition with powers of $r$), `hE5e` (the dictionary of `hE2` with one and the same $g$ serving both all $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ and all $x \in R_2$, with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_x} g\,\iota_0(x)\,g^{-1}$ in the latter case), and `hE5g` ($R_2$ is stable under the main involution).
--
--   The twin order hypotheses. A second maximal order $\Lambda_1^s$ with $R_1 \le \Lambda_1^s$ and $\Lambda_1 \cap \Lambda_1^s = R_1$ (`hΛ₁s`, `hR₁Λ₁s`, `htwin`); an order $R_2' \le \Lambda_1^s$ (`hR₂'`, `hR₂'o`) with $R_2 \le R_2'$ (`hR₂R₂'`) such that every element of $\Lambda_1^s$ enters $R_2'$ after multiplication by a power of $r$ (`hR₂'r`); and $\hat e' \colon R_2' \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base (`hê'`) satisfying `hE6a` (group-law homomorphism and $\Lambda$-equivariance), `hE6one`, `hE6mul`, `hE6int`, `hE6c` (as for $\hat e$, with reduced norms), `hE6d` ($\hat e'$ restricts to $\hat e$ on $R_2$), `hE6star` ($R_2'$ is stable under the main involution), `hE6inj` ($\hat e'$ is injective), and `hE6f`: for every prime $q \ne r$ and every $z \in R_2'$, if $\hat e' z$ annihilates all $q$-torsion points of $A_0$ over every base, then $r^K z \in q\,\Lambda_1^s$ for some $K$.
--
--   The Atkin–Lehner element. A unit $\bar w$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ whose conjugation action preserves $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`), and such that $r^k\bar w \in R_1$ for some $k$ (`hwR`).
--
--   Conclusion. There exist a fake elliptic curve $A_{0,w}$ over $k_0 = Onr/(\pi)$ with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure, morphisms $a_w \colon A_0.A \to A_{0,w}.A$ and $a_w' \colon A_{0,w}.A \to A_0.A$ over the base, an exponent $k_w \in \mathbb N$, morphisms $b_w \colon A_{0,w}.A \to A_0.A$ over the base and $b_w' \colon A_0.A \to A_{0,w}.A$, such that the following hold.
--
--   First, $(a_w, a_w')$ is an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ in the sense of `IsAtkinLehnerQuotientVia`: both maps are homomorphisms for the relative group laws, both are $\Lambda$-equivariant, the two composites are multiplication by $\bar r$ on $A_0$ and on $A_{0,w}$ respectively, a point $P$ of $A_0$ is killed by $a_w$ if and only if it is killed by the $\Lambda$-action of every $m$ with $m\,\bar m = \bar r\,n$ for some integer $n$, and $a_w$ carries points factoring through the level structure of $A_0$ to points factoring through that of $A_{0,w}$.
--
--   Secondly, $(b_w, b_w')$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$ from $A_{0,w}$ to $A_0$, and $b_w$ preserves the level structure.
--
--   Thirdly, the dictionary is extended by $\bar w$: for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}(r)$-action and $\varpi$ in $\operatorname{End}(X_0.F)$ into $M_2(K₀)$ there is a single $g \in GL_2(K₀)$ such that, on the one hand, the clause of `hE2` holds with this $g$ for every $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ (an $\varepsilon$ in the centraliser inducing $e\gamma$ on the formal coordinates $\theta_0$ and with $E_0\varepsilon = r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$), and, on the other hand, there are $\varepsilon_w$ in the centraliser and $k_w' \in \mathbb Z$ such that $\varepsilon_w$ induces on the formal coordinates the composite $a_w$ followed by $b_w$ (for every $k_0$-algebra $B'$, every ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$ and every tuple of elements of $J$) and $E_0\varepsilon_w = r^{k_w'}\, g\,\iota_0(\bar w)\, g^{-1}$.
--
--   This is the step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation package that adjoins the Atkin–Lehner involution at the auxiliary prime $\bar r$ to the endomorphism dictionary relating the quaternionic group $\tilde\Gamma$, the formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ and the special fibre of the fake elliptic curve: the element $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ is realised geometrically by an Atkin–Lehner quotient $a_w$ followed by an $r$-power isogeny $b_w$, compatibly with a single conjugating matrix $g$. It is used in the assembly of the fine-level uniformisation statement with Atkin–Lehner data.
